  "response": "Adjudication between disease and predation begins not with pathology but with the shape of the loss itself. Twenty years of pre-collapse census data provided a demographic baseline for the colony, and that long series of counts shows that the colony declined by 90 percent over three breeding seasons; in other words, nine out of every ten breeding birds were lost across the three breeding seasons. A decline of that steepness narrows the field of plausible mechanisms before any individual bird is examined.\n\nNecropsies of recovered carcasses were performed to identify gross lesions. Scavenging of carcasses obscures the original cause of death. PCR assays on cloacal swabs detect avian influenza viral RNA. Serological surveys of surviving adults measure the prevalence of antibodies indicating prior pathogen exposure. Examination for gross lesions can speak only to the few animals whose remains are retrieved, whereas antibody prevalence among the survivors characterises the living population. Inferential discipline at this stage is strict: a negative PCR result from a single carcass rules out viral infection across the whole population. Although scavengers had obscured the original cause of death in much of the recovered material, amplification of viral RNA from cloacal swabs confirms that at least one recovered carcass tested positive for highly pathogenic avian influenza. Highly pathogenic avian influenza viruses are incapable of infecting wild seabirds, a position that cannot be squared with that positive detection in the remains of a wild seabird.\n\nPredation hypotheses are tested on quite different ground, in the burrows themselves. Camera traps installed at nesting burrows recorded visits by introduced mammals, and that record led to experimental rat eradication in one sector of the reserve, which created a comparison with untreated sectors. Such a design converts an observation into a manipulation: if burrow predation is driving the collapse, breeding output should recover where the rodents have been removed and remain depressed where they have not, and the magnitude of that difference sets an upper bound on the share of the decline attributable to mammals.\n\nA third body of evidence is demographic rather than diagnostic. Mark-recapture data from ringed adults yield annual survival estimates for the colony. The age structure of mortality distinguishes disease outbreaks from predation on eggs and chicks. Taken together, the pathological, experimental and demographic strands seldom point in a single direction with equal force, and the explanation that finally stands is the one compatible with all of them at once: a pathogen signature in the tissues of the dead, a treatment effect in the manipulated sector of the reserve, and a mortality profile whose distribution across ages matches the mechanism being proposed. Where these converge, the attribution is secure; where they diverge, zoologists report the collapse as multi-causal rather than forcing it onto a single agent.",
